Coastal wetlands, such as salt marshes and mangrove forests, act as natural buffers that mitigate storm surge and wave energy through several distinct physical mechanisms. The primary mechanism is frictional drag. As storm-driven water moves through dense vegetation, the stems, leaves, and roots create resistance, slowing the flow velocity. This reduction in speed significantly lowers the kinetic energy of the water, thereby decreasing its destructive force upon reaching inland structures. Additionally, wetlands provide vertical elevation and volume storage. During high tides or storm surges, these ecosystems act like sponges, absorbing excess water and reducing the peak height of the surge that